# The four per-user post writes. Every `api` line is filtered to `post_acks`; the two CONTROLs
# are at the end.
#
# Deliberately absent: everything touching `urgent_mention_count`. `POST /posts` with a
# `metadata.priority` is a **403 on this stack** — measured, in a DM and in town-square — so no
# urgent post exists, `count_urgent_posts_after` always answers 0, and both arms of
# `if self.config().post_priority` give the same body. Those lines would be survivors by
# construction and a survivor whose reason is known in advance is noise, not a finding.
#
# The dispatcher and the two refusals come first: a refusal no test can see serves a request Go
# would have answered differently, with a row already written.
#
# `last_viewed_at_is_stamped_instead_of_derived` is written as a **swap** of the two timestamps
# rather than as `$6` -> `$8`. The one-sided form leaves `$6` referenced nowhere and
# `sqlx::query!` then refuses to compile ("could not determine data type of parameter $6"), which
# the harness reports as a fault rather than a verdict. Every bound parameter must stay used.
#
# `the_user_id_is_validated_before_the_post_id` runs on the **unit** suite. The order is not
# observable on the wire — both refusals share an id and a status, and the parameter name lives
# only in `AppError.params`, which is never serialised ([D-384]) — so it survived the whole parity
# suite. `require_post_id_then_user_id` exists so a unit test can read what a client cannot.
crt_dispatcher_becomes_an_or	crates/mm-app/src/post_unread.rs	let crt_path = collapsed_threads_supported && self.is_crt_enabled_for_user(user_id).await;	let crt_path = collapsed_threads_supported || self.is_crt_enabled_for_user(user_id).await;	api	post_acks
